update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('3f5c68c4-8737-490f-b41b-1ca69027a118'::uuid,'330acba2ebb0026ac2fedbd196b159b8','Overlap with ancient thinkers doesn''t stop the test from running. Ask the historian''s question about a shared belief: did any medieval epistemologist believe it? Yes, so it is in. Where else the belief came from has no effect on whether the definition can sort claims.'),
('01678eae-d0c8-4317-8851-790c8b1813a1'::uuid,'8937dce02c5175a15c8df8778147276a','This is about why they held their epistemological beliefs, not which beliefs they held. The definition only asks whether a medieval epistemologist believed a claim. A belief shaped by "nonepistemological matters" is still one of their epistemological beliefs, and the test still sorts it.'),
('2ab8be91-6574-4c5d-b0dc-d80bbe02a89e'::uuid,'2248ec965f8296519e03e95a18623099','The definition only collects their "epistemological beliefs," and the historian only asks about "some epistemological claim." Passages that are "clearly not about epistemology" are simply left out. Mixed writings make the reading longer, but they don''t stop the test from deciding what is included.'),
('28029a1b-b551-46c4-93ea-97824d1d5775'::uuid,'03a4e6b88539482818e6bf001ad355e7','This is the trap the stimulus warned us about. The historian says that if any medieval epistemologists believed the opposite of a claim, that opposite claim also counts. Contradictions were planned for, so this answer describes something the definition already handles. It doesn''t make the definition any harder to apply.'),
('6ed8926a-96f2-4261-831b-8f6ed4b064c7'::uuid,'0e1454af91135c77ef9d9f2b485ae253','The definition sorts claims by asking whether "any medieval epistemologists" believed them. That test needs an agreed list of who the epistemologists were. If there is "much debate" about which thinkers, "if any," qualify, the test can''t run, and the definition fails to settle what is included, which was the whole problem it promised to solve. The move to repeat: when a definition leans on a group, attack our ability to identify that group.'),
('3ae82b06-98b5-4ef7-a819-629993fad1a7'::uuid,'5af648f434b6219795f28991e1a31c2c','This lowers the stakes, not the chance. The conclusion is only that the drugs might "end up in the general food supply." Harmless drugs in our food are still drugs in our food, so the possibility the advocate raised is untouched.'),
('3ca3bfff-6aa0-45d5-b8e4-5dc7bf7e6f7c'::uuid,'d2ebffa10d06ff747295f550270fd9ac','This is a conditional: no drift means no risk. But nothing tells us drift is actually being prevented, so the risk the advocate describes is still there. If anything, it confirms that drift is the route to contamination, which is the advocate''s own story.'),
('bf754625-bf1f-488f-afde-ff800c3dcbac'::uuid,'478ceedf22c11020fa1af3cf6f47b1a3','Smaller crops are still part of the food supply. The advocate never claimed the drugs would reach the biggest crops, only that they could end up in "the general food supply." Shrinking how much food is involved doesn''t break any link in the chain.'),
('a289da90-b50c-4d22-b9cf-e6c42994d2b3'::uuid,'d5d9767edd4731efc07bd761f0d3d35d','This breaks the last link. Grant everything the advocate says: the pollen drifts, fertilizes the ordinary crop, and turns it into a drug-producing crop. The drugs are still "not present in any part of the plant used for food," so harvesting that crop for food puts no drug into the food supply. The move to repeat: map the chain from cause to feared result, then find the answer that blocks a link the author never supported.'),
('88958a04-d640-4eb8-8590-63e0d3cf36b2'::uuid,'898c1d9690b391f1bf5fafe53d2a759c','Being able to identify a converted crop is not the same as keeping it out of the food supply. To make this weaken, we''d have to add that identified crops are always pulled before harvest, and the answer never says that. As written, the drug could still get into food.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;